WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:13.630
If you’re an adult in these here United
States, you probably have a credit score.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:19.050
And you’ve probably been told, maybe more
than once, to live your life in such a way

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:25.800
that will improve that strange number that
lies somewhere between 300 and 850.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.190
“Build good credit,” you are told, and
financial success will follow.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:36.200
The higher your credit score, the happier
and more secure you will be.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.090
That is not a wholly irrational belief.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:45.539
Credit scores have a pervasive, though often
invisible, set of impacts on our lives.

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:51.559
They are EVERYWHERE today: they’re often
REQUIRED for renting an apartment, for getting

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:55.749
a mortgage, for taking out a loan, even for
getting a job.

00:00:55.749 --> 00:01:00.339
But for a number so important, it is REMARKABLY
arbitrary.

00:01:00.339 --> 00:01:06.330
You’ve probably experienced just how [fingers]
capricious and downright weird your credit

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.610
score can be.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:13.811
Pay off a loan—do the “responsible thing”—and
your credit score may go [pause] DOWN, not

00:01:13.811 --> 00:01:14.811
up.

00:01:14.811 --> 00:01:19.270
But maintain multiple credit cards, use them
frequently, and continually pay installment

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:25.790
loans, your credit score is likely to INCREASE—as
long as you never miss a payment.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.390
It is a very strange system.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:30.649
Believe it or not, it wasn’t always like
this.

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:36.270
This peculiar practice where a “score”
is so powerful in determining so many aspects

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.780
of your life wasn’t always around.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:44.829
In fact, credit scores—also known as FICO
scores, after the company that created them—weren’t

00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:48.420
used to determine loan pricing until 1989.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.380
That’s not that long ago.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.200
How did it get this way?

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:59.350
How did we get to a point where this funny
little number exerts so much power over our

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.350
lives?

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:07.149
To understand, we’ll have to go back a bit,
to a time called the 1970s.

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:13.860
It was the age of bell-bottom jeans and disco
and Gerald Ford god bless him and Dog Day

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:14.860
Afternoon.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.160
Dog Day Afternoon!

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.910
Not just Al Pacino but John Cazale AND Charles
Durning.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:20.910
At-ti-ca!

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:21.910
At-ti-ca!

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.100
Love that movie.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:30.190
But the 1970s was also a time when American
industry was getting rapidly automated and

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:37.360
outsourced, and a lot of people who had previously
made a pretty good living began to lose out.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:43.170
Throughout the 70s, and going into the 80s,
90s, and 2000s, industries like steel production

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:50.460
or auto manufacturing rapidly shed their workers,
and places like Youngstown or Flint or Warren,

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:56.310
that’s my dad’s hometown, were left without
[slow down] a whole lot of support.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:04.550
Manufacturing fell from more than 30 percent
of total employment in 1955 to 22 percent

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:07.920
in 1975, and less than 19 percent by 1985.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:15.900
By 2005, it had fallen to [slow it down] just
barely above 10 percent.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.170
For the most part, these high-wage industrial
jobs with good pensions and benefits were

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.550
replaced by jobs in the growing “service
sector.”

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:29.160
A lot of people in our great national industrial
belt wound up working at places like Walmart

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:34.830
or McDonald’s, which provided no benefits
or pension and wages a lot lower than what

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.970
people were previously making.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:41.180
At the same time as this [professor voice]
process of deindustrialization was unfolding,

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:44.840
a new philosophy was on the rise in government.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:52.960
It was an attitude that prioritized austerity
and deregulation—an approach that was called

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:53.960
“NEOLIBERALISM.”

00:03:53.960 --> 00:04:00.150
As the masses of unemployed or underemployed
workers grew into a “surplus population”

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:06.920
on the margins of the economy, political elites
slashed welfare programs while deregulating

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.200
the industries like banking.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.690
And in 1978, the Supreme Court prevented states
from enforcing laws capping interest rates

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:20.009
for nationally chartered banks, gutting anti-usury
protections for lenders.

00:04:20.009 --> 00:04:27.120
So the ‘70s saw two important trends: first,
deindustrialization and falling living standards

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.320
in much of the United States; and second,
the deregulation of banking and lending.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.100
In the 1980s, these two trends collided.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:41.630
You see, with a new glut of households that
were dependent on low-wage, no-benefit jobs,

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:46.560
people could no longer survive just on their
incomes as they might have been able to before.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:54.000
So they had to turn to the exciting new world
of consumer credit, and debt became a bigger

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.330
and bigger part of working-class life.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:04.150
This led to what the scholar Susanne Soederberg
calls “DEBTFARE”—consumer debt as a

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:08.330
SUBSTITUTE for good jobs and good wages and
a social safety net.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:12.820
Of course, debt has been around for a very
long time, and was already widely used in

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:18.710
the U.S. to finance big purchases like cars,
or college.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:24.600
But as wages collapsed and welfare programs
were cut, MORE and MORE families were forced

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.830
to go deeply into debt JUST TO STAY AFLOAT.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:33.460
The cold, hard reality was that people just
couldn’t live on what they earned, and were

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:38.710
constantly on a treadmill of debt in order
to provide for their family.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:43.730
In the 1980s, banks learned how to take advantage
of this situation.

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:47.610
Before, the credit cards they offered were
a loss leader for them.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.170
But then they realized something important.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:55.060
The way to make money was NOT to target people
who were well-off and could pay off their

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:56.060
debt.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:02.330
It was to target people who were NOT well-off,
who were reliant on debt and couldn’t pay

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:04.070
it all off at once.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:09.230
As one banker said in an interview – and
I quote – “it didn’t require a lot of

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:14.419
investigation to see that the people who paid
[their debt] in full every month were not

00:06:14.419 --> 00:06:15.419
profitable.”

00:06:15.419 --> 00:06:21.870
Banks called these customers [hands] “REVOLVERS”
— people who took on the maximal amount

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:26.680
of debt they could without defaulting, and
who could be charged higher interest rates

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.560
as a result.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.560
Overwhelmingly, of course, these “revolvers”
were living paycheck to paycheck, who needed

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.210
debt in order to get by in the harsh new economy.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:44.240
They became the target – really, the prey
– for an explosion in consumer lending,

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.490
with the plastic credit card at its center.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:51.450
The banks pitched it as a matter of freedom
and flexibility.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:56.699
But for the downwardly-mobile people they
were targeting, it was about necessity.

00:06:56.699 --> 00:07:01.490
This massive expansion of consumer lending
is where the credit score came in.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:06.270
You see, lenders had long employed ways to
discern how “creditworthy” their applicants

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:13.080
were, with arbitrary judgements of whether
they were “shifty” or “of good character.”

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.930
But as corporations amassed huge reams of
information on customers and their behavior,

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:23.199
they now had enough computerized information
for them to COOK UP algorithms about who was

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:25.460
and was not “creditworthy.”

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:29.150
So in 1975, the first FICO score was developed.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:34.230
But the banks weren’t so interested in “trustworthy”
or “creditworthy” lenders.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:41.340
They wanted those “revolvers,” the people
who would make them as much money as possible.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:47.210
And when the first general-purpose FICO score
came out in 1989, that’s what it was designed

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:48.280
around.

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:53.550
Which is why, in 1995, the director of consumer
policy at Experian—one of the biggest credit

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:59.729
rating agencies—admitted: “Modeling just
credit risk is passé.…[The real risk is]

00:07:59.729 --> 00:08:02.310
that a customer is not profitable.”

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:09.320
They were searching for what one banking publication
called “DEBT-PRONE CONSUMERS,” and systematically

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:15.260
targeting working-class people who now needed
debt in order to survive—and who could be

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:18.699
charged higher interest rates as a result.

00:08:18.699 --> 00:08:25.400
So starting in the 1980s, lending to the poor—so-called
“subprime” or “nonprime” lending—grew

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:26.400
rapidly.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:32.060
The “riskier” the borrowers were, the
more interest they could be charged, and the

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:34.579
more money the bank could make.

00:08:34.579 --> 00:08:40.819
That’s why at Wells Fargo, one of the biggest
banks in America, loan officers were incentivized

00:08:40.819 --> 00:08:48.269
for YEARS to aggressively market subprime
loans to Black communities, even for people

00:08:48.269 --> 00:08:52.499
who qualified for “prime” loans at lower
interest rates.

00:08:52.499 --> 00:08:57.570
These victims were the “profitable customers”
the banks were looking for.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:02.480
Wells Fargo called these “GHETTO LOANS,”
and loan officers referred to the individuals

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.009
they were trapping in debt as “mud people.”

00:09:05.009 --> 00:09:13.889
The goal, of course, was never to help them;
it was to MAKE MONEY off them through predatory

00:09:13.889 --> 00:09:14.889
lending.

00:09:14.889 --> 00:09:17.910
The credit score became a crucial part of
this.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:23.899
It is a tool of discipline that makes you
into the ideal customer for the banks.

00:09:23.899 --> 00:09:28.790
That’s why credit scores don’t reflect
actual financial health—things like the

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:32.589
difference between your income and your spending,
or how much money you’ve saved.

00:09:32.589 --> 00:09:39.819
What they DO reflect is YOU being a reliable,
juicy profit source for THEM.

00:09:39.819 --> 00:09:46.779
They’re not looking for trustworthy borrowers,
but people who can be counted on to live in

00:09:46.779 --> 00:09:47.999
debt.

00:09:47.999 --> 00:09:51.660
Because keeping you in debt means PROFIT.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:57.029
Your credit score rewards you NOT for making
decisions that actually help you financially,

00:09:57.029 --> 00:10:02.240
but for taking on the maximum amount of debt
that you can possibly repay.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:07.470
In an economy as unequal and predatory as
ours, credit scores may be “necessary”

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:10.589
in the narrow sense of what our system demands.

00:10:10.589 --> 00:10:15.509
But credit scores, and the entire system of
debt that lies underneath them, are not a

00:10:15.509 --> 00:10:22.100
fact of nature, or eternal like the thespian
artistry of Al Pacino.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:29.800
Credit scores are necessary ONLY in an economy
that cannibalizes the sweat and tears of its

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:35.629
working people, that regulates so much of
our lives through a number reflecting your

00:10:35.629 --> 00:10:38.439
utility to the banking industry.

00:10:38.439 --> 00:10:44.249
You can spend your whole life working on that
number, and many of you will.

00:10:44.249 --> 00:10:51.660
But no matter how high you get it, no matter
how many years of work and sacrifice you spend—you

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.740
will have ALREADY paid the price.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:11:22.309
I’m Chase Madar, wandering law professor,
for the Gravel Institute.

